WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.836
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy, Grammy and award --

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:06.806
Academy Award, excuse me,
winning rapper and actor.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:10.042
He makes his Broadway debut in
"Between Riverside and Crazy,"

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:11.577
which is playing
at the Hayes Theater.

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:14.347
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Common, everybody.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.883
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:21.587 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.587 --> 00:00:28.761 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:30.129
Welcome back.

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:32.098
-Hey, what's going on, Seth?
-I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.633
Thank you for being here
on a show day.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.402
I know you've got to go and be
on the Broadway stage tonight.

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:37.603
-Yes, it's amazing,

00:00:37.603 --> 00:00:39.672
I'm thinking like, wow, I've got
a show tonight.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.741
It's amazing thing to happen,
and I'm here with you.

00:00:41.741 --> 00:00:43.943
-Because this is your --
this your Broadway debut.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:45.378
-Yes.
-The last time you were in

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.446
a play was a school play,
is that accurate?

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:48.914
-Very true, very true.
-Wow.

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:52.152
-"Christmas Carol" when I was
like in second grade.

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:53.720
-Who were you
in "Christmas Carol?"

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.154
-I was Tiny Tim.

00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:56.288
-Oh, well, look at that.

00:00:56.288 --> 00:00:57.590
You've outgrown the role.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.691
-Yes, yes.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.127
-It happens to all our best
Tiny Tims.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.496
-Yeah, and I didn't get a lot
of love for my role in that,

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:06.065
so that didn't help my -- that
didn't help my acting career.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:08.701
-At second grade, you said I'm
going to take a break,

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:11.170
I'm gonna establish myself
in a lot of other mediums...

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:12.238
-Yes, that's it.
-...and then I will

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:13.773
return to the stage.
-Yeah.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.841
My mother kind of orchestrated
that break because

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:18.577
she was like -- she kept talking
about how great

00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:19.979
my friend did in the play.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:21.480
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.783
And really didn't mention much
about my work,

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.118
so I was like, okay, I get it.

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:27.286
-She was like, you know,
Common, Tiny Tim

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:28.854
is supposed to be
a sympathetic character.

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:30.890
-Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:32.491
I was playing him too cool.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:33.559
-Yeah, way too cool.

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:34.860
Your Tiny Tim was way too cool.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.863
Everybody's like he gonna get
knocked down a peg.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:42.936
So you -- this is a play,
wonderful family drama.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.537
It's about a widowed ex-cop.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.206
You play his previously
incarcerated son.

00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:48.508
-Yes.
-Gorgeous play.

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.910
This is a play that had
been off Broadway.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.879
Most of the cast is back.
You're the new kid on the block.

00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:56.282
Even with all your success, does
it feel -- do you feel

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:59.152
like the rookie with this
group of incredible actors?

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.120
-Oh, I feel so much
like the rookie,

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.255
like a freshman in high school,
right?

00:02:03.255 --> 00:02:07.360
Because it's like they have been
together for so long,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.562
and they just know the ropes
of theater, like,

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:10.863
and it's just
an incredible cast.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:12.932
They really like superheroes.

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:16.369
Really, they just -- you know,
everybody might not know

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.605
each actor's name all the time,
but these actors are just --

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:20.906
this cast is phenomenal.

00:02:20.906 --> 00:02:24.377
So I felt like I was joining
a cast of superheroes,

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.178
and I was like, hey,
I got something, too.

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:27.646
But they gave me a little love.

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:29.281
They gave me love
from the beginning,

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:32.384
and then once they saw
how, like, passionate I was,

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:34.219
and I was coming in
understanding

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:35.855
I had a lot to learn.

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:40.693
And no matter what I did before,
they knew more than me

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.094
and they're more
experienced than me.

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:45.431
So I took it from that
perspective, and also just in

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.534
gratitude of being able to be
doing a play, man,

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.036
doing theater, doing Broadway.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.537
Like, I didn't dream of that

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:54.006
when I was on the south side
of Chicago

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.241
thinking I would be --

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.309
-Yeah.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.810
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.714
You obviously -- you're no
stranger to live performance,

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.384
you're no stranger to being on a
stage in front of an audience.

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:06.085
Are there technical
aspects of this

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:09.321
that have shocked you
or surprised you?

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:12.859
-Yeah, I'm almost embarrassed
to say, but the truth is,

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.761
thank God I have, like,
friends who are into theater,

00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:17.630
because a director
friend of mine,

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.966
Owarya, who I work with often,

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:22.301
she said, okay,
a couple of things.

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:23.736
This is before my
first rehearsal.

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:28.140
She said you be on time,
on time, early is on time.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.611
So I was early every day,
but she also said,

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:35.948
do you know where stage --
what downstage and upstage is

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:37.683
and stage left
and stage right.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:38.951
And I really didn't know.

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.319
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.322
So I was grateful she sent me
a diagram with that.

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:45.892
And it's all type of rules,
like signing in.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.893
You sign in when you come into
the theater,

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:50.129
like signing in for work,
like you punching in for work.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.032
And if you don't like --
if I don't leave the building,

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:55.634
I still have to sign in,
meaning we have two

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.370
shows in a day.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.938
I sign in for the first show.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:00.472
Even if I don't leave the
building, I still have

00:04:00.472 --> 00:04:02.008
to sign in for the second show.

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:04.377
It's just theater rules
that I'm just like, wow, okay,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.878
but I love the culture.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.081
-There's no sign-in sheet
at rap concerts?

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.082
-No, no, no.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:11.017
[ Laughter ]

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.785
-Guys, I know we've got
a big rap concert.

00:04:13.785 --> 00:04:15.054
You've got to sign in.

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:16.255
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.224
--Boy, there would
be no concerts.

00:04:19.224 --> 00:04:22.661
-What about -- You know,
obviously you're on stage,

00:04:22.661 --> 00:04:23.796
and you know as a performer,
as a rapper,

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.030
you connect with the audience.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.033
That's got to be a really
important thing.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:29.168
In a show, you want to connect
with them as well,

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.370
but it must be a totally
different tool kit.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.207
-Yeah, no, a live concert
is a whole different thing.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:37.242
Because, you know, when I'm
performing at a live concert,

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:39.511
I'm like come on, say hoo,
yeah, let's rock it.

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:41.914
I'm directly to the crowd.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.917
You know, in a play, it's --
you're playing this character,

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:49.288
and really you're interacting
and connection is with the other

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:50.957
actors on stage.

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:54.326
And the audience is a part
of the play, but you're not

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:57.630
directing your energy or
thoughts towards the audience.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.465
It's towards the other actors.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:02.334
And emotionally,
the places you have to go,

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:03.736
even though I play -- I play --

00:05:03.736 --> 00:05:07.139
"Between Riverside and Crazy"
is really a lot of laughs,

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:09.876
but it also has a lot of emotion
to it and grit.

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:12.578
So to go to those emotional
places is much different

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:16.115
than rocking out on stage.
You know what I mean?

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.951
-Both cases, live performance,
things can go wrong.

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:22.655
Has anything gone wrong on stage
in the play yet?

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:25.791
-Well, I mean, for me, like,
it's a point in the play

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:28.027
where I'm using this -- where
I'm giving something away.

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:30.295
But I'm using a wheelchair.

00:05:30.295 --> 00:05:32.331
And I got stuck in the
wheelchair really quick

00:05:32.331 --> 00:05:34.366
for a minute and couldn't move.

00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:36.235
But I don't know if everybody
noticed it, but I did.

00:05:36.235 --> 00:05:38.004
I kind of laughed to myself.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.706
And sometimes, you know,
in the play I'm making

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:43.142
a peanut butter and jelly
sandwich in one of the scenes,

00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:45.077
and that doesn't
always go right.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.411
[ Laughter ]

00:05:46.411 --> 00:05:51.284
But overall it's just like --
I've been in concerts

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.719
where the sound has went out
and you just have to

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:55.921
rap out loud to people.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:59.224
So nothing makes me too scared,
too nervous,

00:05:59.224 --> 00:06:02.462
but I must say, we have had a
couple of little trip ups.

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:05.331
-You've never had to take out
your map of where stage left

00:06:05.331 --> 00:06:06.431
and stage right is?

00:06:06.431 --> 00:06:08.934
-Not in a rap --
not in a rap show.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.503
-I got a lot more
questions for you.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:12.438
We'll be right back with more
from Common, everybody.

